<exercises xml:id="ex-ImproperIntegrals">
    <exercise>
      <p>
        Explain what it means for an integral to be improper. What kinds of issues are we looking at?
      </p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
      <p>
        Give an example of an integral that is improper due to an unbounded or infinite interval of integration (infinite width).
      </p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
      <p>
        Give an example of an integral that is improper due to an unbounded integrand (infinite height).
      </p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
      <p>
        What does it mean for an improper integral to converge? How does this connect with limits?
      </p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
      <p>
        What does it mean for an improper integral to diverge? How does this connect with limits?
      </p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
      <p>
        Why do we need to use limits to evaluate improper integrals?
      </p>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
      <introduction>
        <p>
          For each of the following improper integrals:
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                Explain why the integral is improper. Be specific, and point out the issues in detail.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Set up the integral using the correct limit notation.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Antidifferentiate and evaluate the limit.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Explain whether the integral converges or diverges.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </introduction>
      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\displaystyle \int_{x=0}^{\infty} \frac{1}{\sqrt{x+1}}\;dx</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            This is an improper integral because of the infinite width of the integral: we cannot use the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus to evaluate this, since the function isn't being considered on a closed interval (or even an open one with finite width).
            <md>
              <mrow>\int_{x=0}^{\infty} \frac{1}{\sqrt{x+1}}\;dx \amp = \lim_{t\to \infty} \int_{x=0}^{x=t} (x+1)^{-1/2}\;dx</mrow>
              <mrow> \amp = \lim_{t\to\infty} 2(x+1)^{1/2}\bigg|_{x=0}^{x=t}</mrow>
              <mrow> \amp = \lim_{t\to\infty} 2(t+1){1/2} - 2</mrow>
              <mrow> \amp = \infty</mrow>
            </md>
            The limit does not exist, and so this integral diverges.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>
      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\displaystyle \int_{x=0}^{\infty} e^{-2x}\;dx</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            This is an improper integral because of the infinite width of the integral: we cannot use the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus to evaluate this, since the function isn't being considered on a closed interval (or even an open one with finite width).
            <md>
              <mrow>\int_{x=0}^{\infty} e^{-2x} \;dx \amp = \lim_{t\to \infty} \int_{x=0}^{x=t} e^{-2x}\;dx</mrow>
              <mrow> \amp = \lim_{t\to\infty} -\frac{1}{2} e^{-2x}\bigg|_{x=0}^{x=t}</mrow>
              <mrow> \amp = \lim_{t\to\infty} -\frac{1}{2e^{2t}} + \frac{1}{2e^0}</mrow>
              <mrow> \amp = \frac{1}{2}</mrow>
            </md>
            The limit exists, and so the integral converges to <m>\frac{1}{2}</m>.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>
      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\displaystyle \int_{x=-1}^{x=3} \dfrac{1}{x+1}\;dx </m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            This integral is improper because at <m>x=-1</m> the integrand function has a vertical asymptote: this unbounded discontinuity violates the requirements of the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus.
            <md>
              <mrow> \int_{x=-1}^{x=3} \dfrac{1}{x+1}\;dx \amp = \lim_{t\to-1^+} \int_{x=t}^{x=3} \dfrac{1}{x+1}\;dx </mrow>
              <mrow> \amp = \lim_{t\to-1^+} \ln|x+1|\bigg|_{x=t}^{x=3}</mrow>
              <mrow> \amp = \lim_{t\to-1^+} \ln|4| - \ln|t+1|</mrow>
              <mrow> \amp = \ln(4) - (-\infty)</mrow>
              <mrow> \amp = \infty</mrow>
            </md>
             The limit does not exist, and so this integral diverges.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>
      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\displaystyle \int_{-\infty}^{x=0} \sqrt{e^x}\;dx</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            This is an improper integral because of the infinite width of the integral: we cannot use the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus to evaluate this, since the function isn't being considered on a closed interval (or even an open one with finite width).
            <md>
              <mrow> \int_{-\infty}^{x=0} \sqrt{e^x}\;dx \amp = \lim_{t\to-\infty}  \int_{x=t}^{x=0} e^{\frac{1}{2}x}\;dx</mrow>
              <mrow> \amp = \lim_{t\to-\infty} \left(2e^{\frac{1}{2}x}\right)\bigg|_{x=t}^{x=0} </mrow>
              <mrow> \amp = \lim_{t\to-\infty} 2e^{0} - 2e^{t/2} </mrow>
              <mrow> \amp = 2</mrow>
            </md>
            The limit exists, and so the integral converges to <m>2</m>.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>
      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\displaystyle \int_{x=2}^{x=8} \dfrac{5}{(x-2)^3}\;dx</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            This integral is improper because at <m>x=2</m> the integrand function has a vertical asymptote: this unbounded discontinuity violates the requirements of the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus.
          </p>
          <p>
            <md>
              <mrow> \int_{x=2}^{x=8} \dfrac{5}{(x-2)^3}\;dx \amp = \lim_{t\to 2^+} \int_{x=t}^{x=8} 5(x-2)^{-3}\;dx</mrow>
              <mrow> \amp = \lim_{t\to 2^+} -\frac{5}{2(x-2)^2} \bigg|{x=t}^{x=8}</mrow>
              <mrow> \amp = \lim_{t\to 2^+} -\frac{5}{2(6)^2} + \frac{5}{2(t-2)^2}</mrow>
              <mrow> \amp = - \frac{5}{72} + \infty</mrow>
              <mrow> \amp = \infty</mrow>
            </md>
            The limit does not exist, and so this integral diverges.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>
      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            <m>\displaystyle \int_{x=1}^{x=12} \dfrac{1}{\sqrt[5]{12-x}}\;dx</m>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            This integral is improper because at <m>x=12</m> the integrand function has a vertical asymptote: this unbounded discontinuity violates the requirements of the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus.
            <md>
              <mrow>\int_{x=1}^{x=12} \dfrac{1}{\sqrt[5]{12-x}}\;dx \amp = \lim_{t\to 12^-} \int_{x=1}^{x=t} (12-x)^{-1/5}\;dx</mrow>
              <mrow> \amp = \lim_{t\to 12^-} -\frac{5(12-x)^{4/5}}{4}\bigg|_{x=1}^{x=t}</mrow>
              <mrow> \amp = \lim_{t\to 12^-} -\frac{5(12-t)^{4/5}}{4} +\frac{5(11)^{4/5}}{4}</mrow>
              <mrow> \amp = \frac{5(11)^{4/5}}{4} </mrow>
            </md>
            The limit exists, and so the integral converges to <m>\dfrac{5(11)^{4/5}}{4} </m>.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
      <statement>
        <p>
          One of the big ideas in probability is that for a curve that defines a probability density function, the area under the curve needs to be 1. What value of <m>k</m> makes the function <m>\dfrac{kx}{(x^2+3)^{5/4}}</m> a valid probability distribution on the interval <m>[0,\infty)</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          We'll set up the integral <m>\displaystyle \int_{x=0}^\infty \frac{kx}{(x^2+3)^{5/4}}\;dx</m> and find the value of <m>k</m> such that:
          <md>
            \int_{x=0}^\infty \frac{kx}{(x^2+3)^{5/4}}\;dx =1
          </md>.
          
          <md>
            <mrow>\int_{x=0}^\infty \frac{kx}{(x^2+3)^{5/4}}\;dx \amp = \lim_{t\to\infty} \int_{x=0}^{x=t} \frac{kx}{(x^2+3)^{5/4}}\;dx</mrow>
            <mrow>u = x^2+3\;\;\; \amp \;\;\;dx = 2x\;du </mrow>
            <mrow> \amp = \frac{k}{2}\lim_{t\to\infty} \int_{u=3}^{u=t^2+3} u^{-5/4}\;du</mrow>
            <mrow> \amp =\frac{k}{2} \lim_{t\to\infty} -\frac{4}{u^{1/4}}\bigg|_{u=3}^{u=t^2+3}</mrow>
            <mrow> \amp = \frac{k}{2} \lim_{t\to\infty} -\frac{4}{(t^2+3)^{5/4}} + \frac{4}{\sqrt[4]{3}}</mrow>
            <mrow> \amp \frac{2k}{\sqrt[4]{3}}</mrow>
          </md>
          Since we are hoping for this integral to be 1, we need <m>k=\frac{\sqrt[4]{3}}{2}</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise>
      <introduction>
        <p>
          Let's consider the integral <m>\displaystyle \int_{x=1}^{\infty} \dfrac{\sqrt{x^2+1}}{x^2}\;dx</m>. This is a difficult integral to evaluate!
        </p>
      </introduction>
      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            First, compare <m>\sqrt{x^2+1}</m> to <m>\sqrt{x^2}</m> using an inequality: which one is bigger?
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            Since <m>k^2+1 \gt k</m>, we know that <m>\sqrt{k^2+1} \gt \sqrt{k^2}</m>.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>
      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Second, use this inequality to compare the function <m>\dfrac{\sqrt{x^2+1}}{x^2}</m> to <m>\dfrac{1}{x}</m> for <m>x\gt 0</m>: which one is bigger? Again, use your inequality from above to help!
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            First, since <m>k\gt 0</m>, we know that <m>\sqrt{k^2} = k</m>. From our work above, we know that <m>\sqrt{k^2+1} \gt k</m>.
            <md>
              <mrow>\sqrt{k^2+1} \amp \gt k</mrow>
              <mrow>\frac{\sqrt{k^2+1}}{k^2} \amp \gt \frac{k}{k^2}</mrow>
              <mrow>\frac{\sqrt{k^2+1}}{k^2} \amp \gt \frac{1}{k}</mrow>
            </md>
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>
      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Now compare <m>\displaystyle \int_{x=1}^\infty \dfrac{\sqrt{x^2+1}}{x^2}\;dx</m> to <m>\displaystyle \int_{x=1}^\infty \dfrac{1}{x}\;dx</m>. Which one is bigger?
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            Since <m>\dfrac{\sqrt{k^2+1}}{k^2} \gt \dfrac{1}{k}</m> on the interval <m>[1,\infty)</m>, we can say that:
            <md>
              \int_{x=1}^{\infty} frac{\sqrt{k^2+1}}{k^2}\;dx \gt \int_{x=1}^{\infty} \frac{1}{k}\;dx
            </md>.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>
      <task>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Explain how we can use this result to make a conclusion about whether our integral, <m>\displaystyle \int_{x=1}^\infty \dfrac{\sqrt{x^2+1}}{x^2}\;dx</m> converges or diverges.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
              <md>
                <mrow>\int_{x=1}^{\infty} \frac{1}{x}\;dx \amp = \lim_{t\to\infty} \int_{x=1}^{x=t} \frac{1}{x}\;dx</mrow>
                <mrow> \amp = \lim_{t\to\infty} \ln|x|\bigg|_{x=1}^{x=t}</mrow>
                <mrow> \amp = \lim_{t\to\infty} \ln(t)</mrow>
                <mrow> \amp = \infty</mrow>
              </md>
              But since we know that
              <md>
                \int_{x=1}^{\infty} frac{\sqrt{k^2+1}}{k^2}\;dx \gt \int_{x=1}^{\infty} \frac{1}{k}\;dx
              </md>
              we know that the integral <m>\displaystyle \int_{x=1}^\infty \dfrac{\sqrt{x^2+1}}{x^2}\;dx</m> must diverge as well: there is no real number that the limit of this integral could converge to that is greater than infinity.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </task>
    </exercise>
</exercises>